A||||7||DaveL||Maybe Too Technical||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||08-23-2000||08:24 AM||||This maybe a little too technical for this forum, but I have a question. I have an 87 SE 2.5 DIS THM 125. Had to buy a new ECM not long back, after going through hell with an idiotic "mechanic" who replaced the original with a used unit. He warrantied the unit when I authorized him to do the work. After replacing it once with another used one, he got hesitant to re warranty it. Went to another mech who put in a new unit, most problems disappeared. My question is this: what would happen if he put in a prom chip for a standard transmission instead of for an auto? I've been having intermitent delayed shift problems, and I'm still hoping it is something other than rebuild time. I've changed the fluid and filter, also had the tranny flush treatment at my local "Grease Monkey" shop. No change. Next I'm going to take off the Throttle Valve Cable and clean and lube it. Then maybe I will take apart the governor assembly and check the gears and such. But could the wrong prom cause these symptoms?||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-23-2000||08:39 AM||||the computers are set for the year of the car and what options it had.. eg. auto/stick/ac/ and DIS(4) or cap and rotor ign(6). if he tossed in a Eprom from a stick odds are your torque converter won't lock  and the car would probly have fuel mapping inaccuracys. but it would still run. just not properly. and just changing the Eprom may not fix the problem.. your alternator may be spiking and cooking the computer (bad voltage regulator) or their may be a short in the wiring to the ECU causing it to choke and die. NOTHINGS TOO technical for this forum... there are engineers on here that could make up a new computer system for our little toys from a Teddy Ruxpin doll... i'm not one of them.. but i'm working on it..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hope this helps a bit.. hey Raydar you see this!??!  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-23-2000||11:59 AM||||The wrong EPROM would affect only the locking torque converter, not shifting. Shift timing is a function of the TV cable and the governor.<P>The shift light and the TC lock circuits wire to the same ECM terminal. If you have a stick PROM in an auto car the TC would only try to lock in when the shift light comes on. (And then only in 3rd because there's another switch in the tranny that keeps it from locking in any other gear.)<P>The quick test is the same as looking for TC lock problems. Get the car moving at a steady speed over 35mph. Tap the brake pedal and watch the tach. If the TC is working right the tach will jump about 200 rpm. It will drop a soon as you let off the pedal. you only need press the pedal about an inch. you don't want to actually apply the brakes just activate the TCC disable switch.<P>If this test works then you have a PROM for an automatic.<P>There are 2 ways to check the PROM. read the lable off the PROM itself and read the ID number out of it using an ECM scanner. You may need both numbers.<P>There are several PROMs for the DIS motor. Stick and Automatic aren't the only differances, but their the main ones.<P>There are some common issues with used ECMs but I also agree with 1fst that you need to check the rest of the electrical system just to be safe. <P>The big issue with used ECMs is that they are handled pretty rough more often than not. That results in the connectors getting knocked around and craking the solder joints.<P>Another issue is static electricity. The used ECM needs to be kept in anti static packaging to prevent it getting zapped in transit. Static can kill an ECM out right but what happens more often is that it weakens parts so they fail some time later.<P>The battery also has to be disconnected anytime an ECM is connected/disconnected from the harness.||||reg||1
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||08-23-2000||01:17 PM||||Ogre, this is a bit off the subject, but what would happen if someone where to keep the battery connected when they connected/disconnected the ECM?<P>Would the ECM take an immediate puke or would it just give it a good shock and still be all right?  I imagine that it can't be any good for it, and I know better to disconnect the battery anytime I work with anything electrical.  Would it be like unplugging your computer with it on?  Where it wouldn't be good for it, but the computer does its scandisk thing, then runs ok.<P>  The reason why I ask is because I've had to replace the entire harness of my 84 SE.  A previous owner or shop (master electrician with household twistee connectors) hacked up the entire harness replacing the engine.  I've replaced the entire harness from another 84 with the same options.  Lot of work, but I've added power everything now and the car runs good and wires don't heat up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I guess it's just something I'm wondering if it's happened to my ECM before even though I believe everything is fine.  Would there be any signs that it has, check engine light or a code etc?<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaveL||08-23-2000||01:53 PM||||Sorry guys! I didn't mean too technical for any of you, I meant too technical for me to understand. I guess then I need to go ahead and check the throttle valve cable and the governor. I have a complete governor assembly from a THC 125 that was used on a 2.8. Would they be interchangeable? Don't know what year the THM 125 was from. I can't seem to get the VSS apart from the governor cover. There is a rather thick wire that comes up, bends at a 90 degree angle then again, making a sort of squared off end. Haynes manual says remove retaining clip, see illustration, of which there is none of course.||||reg||6
Z||000005||theogre||08-23-2000||11:23 PM||||Any number of types of damage can be done to an ECM that is unplugged hot. Some things will kill it outright, others can sneek up on you later. No easy way to tell untill it's happened exactly what the result will be. Maybe it will set a code maybe it'll just croak.<P>Your starting point should have been the TV cable.<P>The V6 governor may have diferant springs. That could be a problem. I doubt the governor is bad. Ususally the only thing that happens to these is they get crap stuck in the valve. It's pretty unusual to actually break it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||08-24-2000||08:25 AM||||Ok, I believe my ECM is ok, for now anyway, was just wondering what might or could happen. <P>Thanks Ogre,<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaveL||08-24-2000||11:50 AM||||Ok Governor, the throttle valve cable it is. I see how it somes loose from the wheel at the throttle body end, and I see the one bolt on the valve body end. Should be pretty easy to remove I guess. I'm gonna be daring and try it. As I recall from the other tranny I got the gov. assembly off, there is a small steel wire sticking up with a sort of hook on the end, where you take off the cable. The one I was looking at couldn't fall into the case, and seemed to have a little spring tension on it. Too bad the rest of it was played. A friend gave me an entire THM 125 from an older Fiero, but when I took off the pan I found it was half full of water and discolored fluid. There was what looked like a lot of rust too. Must have been left out in the weather too long. Had to let the trash man take it. Oh well... will let you know if I find a problem with the cable.||||reg||10
